insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-z-score-and-its-ceiling', 1, 'Which standard deviation does `zScores` divide by when the caller leaves the choice unset?', '["The population standard deviation, dividing by n, because the readings are all the data there is.", "The raw median absolute deviation, so that a single glitch cannot inflate the spread it is measured by.", "The sample standard deviation, dividing by n - 1, as NIST/SEMATECH 1.3.5.17 defines the z-score.", "Whichever of the two gives the larger z, so the test is as sensitive as the data allow."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The z-score module states z = (x - mean) / s with the sample standard deviation (n - 1) by default, as NIST/SEMATECH 1.3.5.17 defines it. The population SD is available only when the caller asks for it, the MAD belongs to the modified z-score, and the engine never picks a spread by the result it gives.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-z-score-and-its-ceiling', 2, 'EKENE-3''s ten gauge readings have a mean of 215.250000 and a sample SD of 8.732220. What z does `zScores` return for entry 7, the glitch at 240.100000?', '["2.845783", "2.999718", "2.846050", "186.162000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Entry 7 sits 2.845783 sample standard deviations from the mean. The figure 2.999718 is its z against the population SD, 2.846050 is the ceiling for ten values, and 186.162000 is the modified z-score from the next module.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-z-score-and-its-ceiling', 3, 'With the sample standard deviation, what is the largest absolute z that any value in a series of n values can reach?', '["sqrt(n - 1) for either SD", "No limit: a value can sit as far out as you like", "3, since the engine caps every z at its threshold", "(n - 1) / sqrt(n)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The z-score module gives the ceiling (n - 1) / sqrt(n) for the sample SD. The ceiling sqrt(n - 1) belongs to the population SD, which the engine names in its basis block when that choice is made. However far out a value sits, it drags the mean and widens the SD, and the engine caps nothing at the threshold.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-z-score-and-its-ceiling', 4, 'On the ten gauge readings the engine returns `maxPossibleAbsZ` 2.846050 and `thresholdReachable` false, with 0 flags at 3. What does that zero say?', '["That the gauge readings are clean, because the engine examined each of them and flagged none.", "Nothing about whether the series holds an outlier: no value in ten can pass 3 with the sample SD.", "That entry 7 was dropped before the test ran, which is why the largest z falls short of 3.", "That the engine refused the call for a short series and returned a default result in its place."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The z-score module: at n = 10 no value can pass |z| > 3 with the sample SD, however wild. A zero from a test that could not fire is silence and carries no verdict. Entry 7 was in the call and reached 2.845783, and the engine returned a result with no refusal.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-z-score-and-its-ceiling', 5, 'In the ceiling table, what is the smallest sample size at which a z beyond 3 becomes reachable with the sample SD?', '["10 values, where the ceiling of 2.846050 lies close to 3.", "11 values, where the ceiling is 3.015113.", "12 values, ceiling 3.175426.", "20 values, where the ceiling is 4.248529 and 3 is clear."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The table reads false at 10 values, ceiling 2.846050, and true at 11, ceiling 3.015113. At 12 it is true as well, and 20 values is well past the point where 3 can be reached.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-z-score-and-its-ceiling', 6, 'The caller switches the gauge call to the population standard deviation. What changes?', '["The mean moves away from 215.250000, because the population SD is taken about a different centre.", "The SD rises above 8.732220, so entry 7''s z drops further inside the threshold.", "Entry 7 is flagged at last, because its z against the population SD now clears the threshold of 3.", "The SD falls to 8.284111 and entry 7''s z rises to 2.999718, and nothing is flagged at 3."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The mean is 215.250000 in both columns of the z-score module; only the spread changes. Dividing by n gives the smaller SD, 8.284111, so every z grows, and entry 7 reaches 2.999718, still short of 3, with 0 flags.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-z-score-and-its-ceiling', 7, 'Nine zeros and a one are run with `sd: ''population''` at a stated threshold of 2.9. What ceiling does the engine report?', '["3.000000, which its basis names sqrt(n - 1), population SD.", "2.846050, the ceiling for ten values under either choice of SD.", "3.015113, the sample SD ceiling that the table gives for eleven values.", "2.999718, the population z of the gauge glitch in its ten readings."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine notes: the ceiling follows the chosen standard deviation. With the population SD the ten values reach 3.000000, `thresholdReachable` is true and 1 flag fires at 2.9. The sample SD on the same values stops at 2.846050; the other two figures belong to other rows.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-z-score-and-its-ceiling', 8, 'EKENE-7''s fourteen core plugs could reach a z of 3.474396. The fractured plug at entry 8 reaches 2.985356 and nothing is flagged. Why?', '["Fourteen plugs sit below the ceiling, so no value in them could pass a z of 3 with the sample SD.", "The engine uses the population SD for core data, which shrinks every z in the set below 3.", "The plug is part of its own spread: the sample SD is 0.019859 with it and 0.010574 without it.", "Entry 8 is not the plug farthest from the mean, so its z is not the largest one in the set."]'::jsonb, 2, 'At fourteen values 3 is reachable, so the ceiling is not the cause. The z-score module prints the sample SD at 0.019859 with all fourteen and 0.010574 with entry 8 left out: the plug widens the yardstick that measures it. The default stays the sample SD, and entry 8 carries the largest z, 2.985356.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-z-score-and-its-ceiling', 9, 'Measured against the mean and sample SD of the other thirteen plugs, where does the fractured plug at entry 8 sit?', '["2.985356", "2.066042", "5.890633", "6.038080"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The z-score module derives 6.038080 against the other thirteen. Measured against a spread that includes itself the plug reads 2.985356, which is what `zScores` returns. 2.066042 is the largest z among the thirteen, and 5.890633 is the modified z-score.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-z-score-and-its-ceiling', 10, 'With the fractured plug left out, what do the thirteen remaining plugs return?', '["A mean of 0.217154, a sample SD of 0.010574 and a largest z of 2.066042, with 0 flags.", "A mean of 0.221714 and a sample SD of 0.019859, the same as the full set.", "A largest z of 6.038080 at a second plug, which the fractured plug had been hiding.", "A refusal, since thirteen values sit under the ceiling and no z can be computed."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The z-score module prints the second row: 0.217154, 0.010574 and 2.066042, and no flag. The full-set figures are 0.221714 and 0.019859. The 6.038080 belongs to entry 8 itself against the thirteen, and a series of thirteen raises no refusal.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-z-score-and-its-ceiling', 11, 'A report states that the gauge glitch sits ''almost three sigma out''. What does this course require the report to add?', '["A P label for the reading, so readers know which tail it sits in.", "Nothing, since sigma always means the sample SD.", "The source of that sigma: the sample SD of the ten readings, 8.732220.", "The same distance converted to a percentage of the mean, so it reads without units."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The course vocabulary makes sigma a standard deviation with its source named: sample SD, 1.4826 x MAD or another stated source. Here the z was measured against the sample SD, 8.732220. The course uses no P label at all, and a percentage of the mean is a different quantity.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-z-score-and-its-ceiling', 12, 'Which reading of a flag from `zScores` does this course accept?', '["The value is wrong, since only a faulty reading or a failed sensor can sit that far from the mean of the rest.", "The value sits beyond the threshold in sample SDs from the sample mean, and that is a question about it.", "The value is removed from the series and the mean and SD are recomputed without it.", "The value is one of the defects the Ekene generator planted."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The course''s word for such a value names its rule, and the flag asks a question about the reading. `zScores` returns flags and leaves the series untouched, and a flag need not land on a planted defect: on the gauge the planted glitch is not even flagged by this rule.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-z-score-and-its-ceiling', 13, 'Eight core plugs are screened with `zScores` at 3. What is the most that any z among them can reach with the sample SD?', '["2.474874", "2.846050", "1.788854", "3.015113"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The ceiling table prints 2.474874 at n = 8, below 3, so no plug in eight can be flagged at that threshold. 2.846050 is the row for 10 values and 1.788854 the row for 5; the ceiling depends on n and the standard deviation chosen, and 3.015113 needs eleven values.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-z-score-and-its-ceiling', 14, 'The gauge glitch reaches 2.999718 against the population SD. Why is it still not flagged at a threshold of 3?', '["The engine falls back to the sample SD at ten values.", "The engine rounds each z to two decimals first, so the glitch compares as 3.", "Values equal to the ceiling are exempt from flagging.", "A flag needs |z| strictly beyond 3, and 2.999718 sits just below it."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The z-score module flags a value when |z| > 3. 2.999718 is below that, and the population column shows 0 flags. The engine accepts the population SD and compares the unrounded figure; the population ceiling at ten values is 3.000000.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-z-score-and-its-ceiling', 15, 'Twenty daily rates are screened with `zScores` at 3 and the sample SD. Can any of them be flagged?', '["No: twenty values put the ceiling at 2.846050, below 3.", "No: with the sample SD the ceiling stays below 3 at any n.", "Yes: the ceiling at 20 values is 4.248529, above 3.", "Only with the population SD, whose ceiling at 20 is 3.000000."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The ceiling table prints 4.248529 at 20 values, reachable at 3. 2.846050 is the ten-value row, the ceiling first passes 3 at eleven values, and 3.000000 is the population ceiling for the ten-value case in the engine notes.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-median-and-the-mad', 1, 'What does the engine return as the MAD of a series?', '["The raw median of |x - median|, with no scale factor applied.", "The median of |x - median| multiplied by 1.4826, so that it reads as a standard deviation.", "The mean of |x - mean|.", "The median of the squared deviations from the median, taken before any square root."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The median and MAD module defines the MAD as the raw median of |x - median|. The 1.4826 scale belongs to the Hampel window, which multiplies the raw MAD by it; an average of distances from the mean is a different statistic that one glitch would drag.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-median-and-the-mad', 2, 'Which two gauge statistics stay the same when the glitch at entry 7 is left out?', '["The mean and the median.", "The sample SD and the MAD.", "The mean and the sample SD.", "The median, 212.500000, and the MAD, 0.100000."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The median and MAD module prints both rows: the median is 212.500000 and the MAD 0.100000 either way. The mean moves from 215.250000 to 212.488889 and the sample SD from 8.732220 to 0.126930 when entry 7 goes.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-median-and-the-mad', 3, 'What modified z-score does the engine return for the gauge glitch at entry 7?', '["186.159450", "186.162000", "2.845783", "5.890633"]'::jsonb, 1, 'M = 0.6745 (x - median) / MAD with the median 212.500000 and the MAD 0.100000 gives the engine''s 186.162000. 186.159450 is the same reading with 1 / 1.4826 in place of 0.6745, 2.845783 is the ordinary z, and 5.890633 is the core plugs'' largest M.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-median-and-the-mad', 4, 'The engine multiplies by 0.6745 in the modified z-score. What is that constant?', '["The reciprocal of Tukey''s fence multiplier 1.5, carried to four decimals.", "A Petrolord choice tuned on the Ekene field data so that the gauge glitch clears the 3.5 threshold.", "The figure as Iglewicz and Hoaglin print it and NIST/SEMATECH 1.3.5.17 reproduces it.", "The ratio of the population SD to the sample SD at ten readings."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine''s export list gives `MODIFIED_Z_SCALE` 0.674500 with Iglewicz and Hoaglin (1993), as NIST prints it, as its source, and the median and MAD module says the engine uses 0.6745 as printed. The reciprocal of 1.4826 is 0.674491, a different number, and the constant was not fitted to any dataset.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-median-and-the-mad', 5, 'On the EKENE-7 core plugs the median is 0.215500 and the MAD 0.007500. What does the modified z-score report?', '["A largest |M| of 5.890633 at entry 8, the only entry flagged.", "A largest |M| of 2.985356 at entry 8, below 3.5, so nothing is flagged.", "Entries 2 and 8 flagged, since both sit far above the median of the fourteen plugs.", "A refusal: the plugs are too few."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The median and MAD module prints the core row: largest |M| 5.890633, entry 8 flagged. 2.985356 is the ordinary z of the same plug. Entry 2 is flagged only in the copy with a second high plug, and fourteen plugs raise no refusal.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-median-and-the-mad', 6, 'The series 5, 5, 5, 6, 7 is passed to `modifiedZScores`. What does the engine do?', '["It falls back to the sample SD and reports the result as a modified z-score.", "It returns an M of zero for every entry, since each sits within one MAD.", "It flags 6 and 7, the only values that are not equal to the median.", "It refuses, naming the field `values`, because the MAD is zero."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The refusal table and the median and MAD module: three of the five values equal the median, so the MAD is 0 and the engine refuses with "values have MAD = 0: more than half the present values equal the median, so the modified z-score is undefined". The engine notes lists a fallback for a MAD of zero as not built.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-median-and-the-mad', 7, 'The series 5, 5, 5, 6, 7 has a spread, yet its median absolute deviation is zero. What makes it zero?', '["Three of the five values sit on the median, 5, so the middle absolute deviation is 0.", "Every present value is the same, which is the only way a MAD can ever reach zero.", "Five values are too few for a MAD, so the engine sets it to zero by rule on a short series.", "The mean and the median coincide, so every deviation cancels out."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The median of 5, 5, 5, 6, 7 is 5 and three of the five values equal it, so three of the five absolute deviations are 0 and the middle one is 0. The MAD is 0 and the modified z-score refuses. The values 6 and 7 show that a spread in the data does not prevent it; the length of the series and the mean play no part, and the engine sets nothing by rule.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-median-and-the-mad', 8, 'What is the engine''s own label for a value whose modified z-score is beyond 3.5?', '["A confirmed outlier, since the modified z-score is the robust test", "An invalid reading", "A potential outlier", "A planted defect, to be removed before any statistic is computed"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The course vocabulary records the engine''s word for the modified z as "potential outlier", from Iglewicz and Hoaglin as NIST prints them. A flag is a question about the value; invalid is the range check''s language, and planted defects are the generator''s list.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-median-and-the-mad', 9, 'With 1 / 1.4826 in place of 0.6745, the gauge glitch would read 186.159450 against the engine''s 186.162000. What does that do to the flag?', '["Nothing: both figures sit far beyond 3.5, so entry 7 is flagged either way.", "It removes the flag at entry 7.", "It adds a flag at entry 1, the highest of the ordinary readings, which the reciprocal now reaches.", "It changes the engine''s refusal."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The median and MAD module derives 186.159450 with 0.674491 against the engine''s 186.162000. Both are enormous next to 3.5, so the flag at entry 7 stands. Nothing about entry 1 or any refusal changes with the constant.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-median-and-the-mad', 10, 'Why does the modified z-score catch the gauge glitch that the ordinary z-score missed?', '["The modified z-score has a lower threshold than 3, so a smaller distance is enough to raise a flag on the gauge.", "The modified z-score divides by the population SD, which is smaller than the sample SD on the gauge readings.", "The modified z-score leaves the largest reading out before it computes anything, so entry 7 cannot mask itself.", "The median and the MAD are the same with or without entry 7, so the glitch is measured against statistics it did not move."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The median and MAD module: median 212.500000 and MAD 0.100000 in both rows, while the sample SD falls from 8.732220 to 0.126930 without entry 7. The threshold is 3.5, higher than 3, the modified z uses no standard deviation, and every present value takes part.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-median-and-the-mad', 11, 'The modified z-score and the Hampel window both build a spread from the raw MAD. How does each use it?', '["Both multiply the raw MAD by 1.4826 first, and the modified z then scales its threshold of 3.5 by 0.6745.", "The modified z divides by 1.4826 x MAD and Hampel by 0.6745 x MAD, the two scales swapped between them.", "The modified z multiplies the deviation by 0.6745 and divides by the raw MAD; Hampel multiplies the raw MAD by 1.4826.", "Neither uses the raw MAD: both take the sample SD of the values nearest the median and call it a MAD."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The median and MAD module gives M = 0.6745 (x - median) / MAD with the raw MAD, and says Hampel''s scale, 1.4826 x MAD, is the same idea from the other side. The engine''s export list gives the two constants separately, `MODIFIED_Z_SCALE` 0.674500 and `HAMPEL_MAD_SCALE` 1.482600.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-median-and-the-mad', 12, 'The mean of the ten gauge readings is 215.250000. What is the mean of the nine readings left once entry 7 is removed?', '["212.500000", "215.250000", "212.400000", "212.488889"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The median and MAD module prints the derived mean with entry 7 left out as 212.488889, computed by lib/stats `mean`. 212.500000 is the median of either set, and 215.250000 is the mean with the glitch still in.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-median-and-the-mad', 13, 'Once the glitch is dropped, how widely do the nine ordinary gauge readings scatter by the sample SD?', '["0.100000", "0.126930", "8.732220", "8.284111"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The median and MAD module prints 0.126930 for the nine readings. 0.100000 is the MAD, 8.732220 the sample SD with the glitch in, and 8.284111 the population SD of all ten. One reading takes the sample SD from 0.126930 to 8.732220.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-median-and-the-mad', 14, 'Where does the Hampel scale 1.4826, the other constant that works with a raw MAD, come from?', '["Iglewicz and Hoaglin, as NIST/SEMATECH prints it.", "The petrophysics conditioning engine, despikeHampel.", "Tukey, as the multiplier of the inner fence.", "A Petrolord choice, fitted to the gauge readings."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine''s export list gives `HAMPEL_MAD_SCALE` 1.482600 with the petrophysics conditioning engine''s despikeHampel as its source. Iglewicz and Hoaglin supply 0.6745 and the threshold 3.5, Tukey''s multiplier is 1.5, and no constant was fitted to Ekene data.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-median-and-the-mad', 15, 'A value has a modified z-score of exactly 3.5. Is it labelled?', '["Yes. A value on the threshold counts as beyond it, so the label fires at 3.5 exactly.", "Only if the MAD is below 0.100000, which makes a figure on the threshold unstable.", "No. The label needs |M| strictly beyond 3.5.", "The engine refuses, because a statistic equal to its threshold is ambiguous."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The median and MAD module labels a potential outlier when |M| > 3.5. The comparison is strict, as it is for the z-score, the Tukey fences and the Hampel threshold, so a value exactly on 3.5 carries no label and no refusal is raised.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-quartiles-and-fences', 1, 'Which quantile rule places the position at h = p(N + 1)?', '["R7", "R8", "R6", "R9"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The quantile rules lesson: h = p(N + 1) for R6, 1 + p(N - 1) for R7 and p(N + 1/3) + 1/3 for R8. The engine implements those three only, as NIST/SEMATECH 7.2.6.2 states them.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-quartiles-and-fences', 2, 'Which quartile rule does `iqrFences` use when the caller leaves `method` unset?', '["R7, the default of Excel, R and numpy.", "R6, as NIST''s own rule.", "R8, as the fence rule.", "Whichever rule gives the widest IQR on the series passed."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The fences lesson: quartiles by the stated rule, R7 by default, and the quantile rules lesson names R7 as the default of Excel, R and numpy. R6 is NIST''s rule and is available by name, and the engine never picks a rule from the data.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-quartiles-and-fences', 3, 'What is the first quartile of the EKENE-7 water sand gamma ray, entries 130 to 199, by R8?', '["31.817500", "31.947500", "38.725833", "31.825833"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The quantile rules lesson prints Q1 as 31.817500 by R6, 31.947500 by R7 and 31.825833 by R8. 38.725833 is the R8 third quartile.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-quartiles-and-fences', 4, 'On the water sand gamma ray, which rule gives the widest interquartile range?', '["R8, at 6.900000.", "R6, at 6.920000.", "R7, at 6.695000.", "None: all three give one IQR."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The quantile rules lesson prints the derived IQR as 6.920000 by R6, 6.900000 by R8 and 6.695000 by R7. R6 is the widest, R8 close behind, R7 the narrowest; the three are not equal even on seventy samples.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-quartiles-and-fences', 5, 'Where does `iqrFences` put the upper Tukey fence for the water sand gamma ray at its default settings?', '["49.117500", "58.727500", "48.685000", "38.642500"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The fences lesson prints the default upper fence as 48.685000, Q3 + 1.5 IQR on the R7 quartiles. 49.117500 is the R6 upper fence at k 1.5, 58.727500 the R7 fence at k 3, and 38.642500 the R7 third quartile itself.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-quartiles-and-fences', 6, 'A colleague reports the water sand''s upper fence at R7 and k 1.5 as 49.117500. What went wrong?', '["That is the R6 fence; at R7 and k 1.5 the upper fence is 48.685000.", "Nothing; that is the R7 fence at the defaults.", "That is the far out fence at k 3 on the same R7 quartiles, which reads 58.727500 on the table.", "That is the whole-log upper fence."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The fences lesson prints the R6, k 1.5 upper fence as 49.117500 and the default R7, k 1.5 fence as 48.685000. The R7, k 3 fence is 58.727500 and the whole-log fence 186.810000, so the colleague''s figure belongs to R6 alone.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-quartiles-and-fences', 7, 'The caller changes the water sand run from R7 to R6, keeping k 1.5. What happens?', '["The IQR narrows, the fences move in to 21.905000 and 48.685000, and a second entry is flagged beside 170.", "The fences stay where they were, because k sets the fences and the quartile rule only labels the result.", "Entry 170 drops inside the wider R6 fences, and the run returns no flag at all on the water sand.", "The IQR widens to 6.920000, the fences move out to 21.437500 and 49.117500, and entry 170 is still the only flag."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The fences lesson prints R6, k 1.5 with IQR 6.920000 and fences 21.437500 and 49.117500, flagging entry 170, against the defaults'' 6.695000, 21.905000 and 48.685000. The rule moves the quartiles and so the fences, and the spike at 90.590000 sits beyond either upper fence.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-quartiles-and-fences', 8, 'Keeping the default R7 quartiles, an analyst asks for far out fences by passing k 3. Which outputs move?', '["The quartiles as well, because k is applied to the positions before the quartiles are computed.", "Only the fences, which move to 11.862500 and 58.727500; the quartiles and IQR are unchanged.", "The IQR doubles, and the fences then sit 1.5 of the doubled IQR outside the quartiles.", "Nothing is flagged any more, since the spike at entry 170 lies inside the far out fences."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The fences lesson prints R7, k 3 with Q1 31.947500, Q3 38.642500 and IQR 6.695000, the same as at k 1.5, and fences 11.862500 and 58.727500. Entry 170 is still flagged, since 90.590000 lies beyond 58.727500.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-quartiles-and-fences', 9, 'Run at the defaults on the whole EKENE-7 gamma ray log, shale and sand together, the fences are -57.070000 and 186.810000 and flag nothing. Why is the spike at entry 170 missed?', '["R7 falls back to R6 on a long log.", "Long series get far out fences at k 3.", "The spread includes the shale, so 90.590000 falls inside the fences.", "A fence cannot flag a value inside a sand."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The fences lesson: on the whole log the spike reads 90.590000, inside a spread that includes the shale. The rule is R7 on any length, k stays 1.5 unless the caller states another, and the fences know nothing of lithology.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-quartiles-and-fences', 10, 'The golden case iqr-exactly-on-both-fences runs -4, 2, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 6, 12 at R7 and k 1.5. How many entries are flagged?', '["0, since -4 and 12 sit exactly on the fences.", "2, the lowest and highest values, which reach the fences.", "1, the 12 alone, since the upper fence is the only one reached.", "No count: the engine refuses a series whose extremes are symmetric."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The fences lesson: Q1 2, Q3 6, IQR 4, fences -4 and 12, and 0 flags. A value strictly outside a fence is flagged, so a value on the fence is inside. The engine returns a result, with no refusal.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-quartiles-and-fences', 11, 'A caller passes k = 0 to `iqrFences`. What does the engine do?', '["It returns fences on the quartiles.", "It uses the default 1.5 in its place.", "It returns the quartiles and IQR with no fences and no flags, since a zero k builds no fence.", "It refuses, naming the field `k`: \"k must be a finite number above zero\"."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The refusal table lists the refusal for a multiplier of zero with the field `k` and the message "k must be a finite number above zero". The engine refuses by name: it neither swaps in 1.5 nor returns quartiles without fences.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-quartiles-and-fences', 12, 'On the NIST silicon wafer resistivities, what 0.9 quantile does the engine return by R7?', '["95.198070", "95.197243", "95.195680", "95.1981"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The quantile rules lesson prints 95.198070 by R6, 95.195680 by R7 and 95.197243 by R8, each equal to its golden. 95.1981 is the figure NIST prints for R6.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-quartiles-and-fences', 13, 'How should a report name the water sand''s first quartile under this course''s vocabulary?', '["By an exceedance label of the kind reserves reports use, so readers know which tail is meant.", "As the first quartile by R7, its probability and its rule.", "As a fixed sorted value of the sand, the same under any rule.", "As a percentile with no rule, since the three rules round to the same figure."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The course vocabulary: a quantile is named by a stated probability and a stated rule, R6, R7 or R8, and no P label is used anywhere. The three rules give 31.817500, 31.947500 and 31.825833 on the sand, so the rule has to be named.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-quartiles-and-fences', 14, 'The R7 lower fence on the water sand is 21.905000. How is it formed?', '["The median minus 1.5 IQR.", "Q1 minus 1.5 sample SDs.", "Q3 minus 1.5 IQR.", "Q1 minus 1.5 times the IQR: 31.947500 less 1.5 x 6.695000."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The fences lesson: the fences are Q1 - k IQR and Q3 + k IQR. With the R7 quartiles, Q1 31.947500 and IQR 6.695000 at k 1.5 give the lower fence 21.905000. A fence is built from the quartiles, never from a median or a standard deviation.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-quartiles-and-fences', 15, 'What does the engine do when a quantile position h falls below the first sorted value or beyond the last?', '["It clamps the quantile to the minimum or the maximum.", "It extrapolates past the end.", "It refuses, naming p.", "It switches to R6."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The quantile rules lesson: with h = k + d the quantile is Y[k] + d (Y[k+1] - Y[k]) on the ordered values, clamped to the minimum and maximum. No refusal and no change of rule is involved.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-a-moving-window', 1, 'How many samples does a Hampel window hold at halfWindow 3, away from the ends of the series?', '["3", "7", "6", "4"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Hampel module: the window is 2 x halfWindow + 1 samples centred on the sample, so halfWindow 3 gives 7. Four is what the window at entry 0 holds once it is truncated at the top of the log.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-a-moving-window', 2, 'At entry 70 of the gamma ray the window MAD is 7.700000 and the engine returns a threshold of 34.248060. How is that threshold built?', '["nSigma x raw MAD: 3 x 7.700000.", "3.5 x MAD / 0.6745.", "nSigma x the window''s sample SD.", "nSigma x 1.4826 x MAD: 3 x 1.4826 x 7.700000."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The Hampel module flags a sample when |x - window median| > nSigma x 1.4826 x MAD of the window, and the threshold column prints 34.248060 for entry 70''s MAD of 7.700000 at nSigma 3. The 1.4826 is `HAMPEL_MAD_SCALE`, the petrophysics conditioning engine''s value.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-a-moving-window', 3, 'Entry 145 is flagged at a deviation of only 7.240000 gAPI, while entry 70 needs far more. Why?', '["Each window has its own MAD: entry 145''s is 0.830000, giving a threshold of 3.691674.", "Entry 145 sits in the upper shale, with a tighter nSigma.", "Entry 145 lies close to the log''s end, where the window is truncated and the threshold halves.", "The engine lowers the threshold for any sample below its window median, and entry 145 reads low."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The Hampel module prints entry 145 with window MAD 0.830000 and threshold 3.691674, against entry 70''s 7.700000 and 34.248060. nSigma is 3 on every row, entry 145 sits in the water sand far from either end, and the rule is the same on both sides of the median.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-a-moving-window', 4, 'On the whole gamma ray channel the z-score flags nothing: entry 70 reads z 1.058062 and entry 170 reads 0.900269. Why does the global rule miss both spikes?', '["Two hundred and forty samples put the z ceiling below 3, so no value on the channel can be flagged.", "The spikes are sixty gAPI below the sand, and the z-score looks only at values above the mean.", "The global mean and SD mix shale and sand, so a sand spike to 95.420000 gAPI looks like shale.", "The z-score skips any sample that a Hampel window has already flagged on the same channel."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The Hampel module: a sand spike to 95.420000 gAPI looks like shale to a global mean and SD. The ceiling rises with n, 4.248529 already at 20 values, the spikes were added to the sand, the z test is on |z|, and no rule skips another''s flags.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-a-moving-window', 5, 'How many of the eleven Hampel flags on the gamma ray at halfWindow 3 and nSigma 3 are planted defects?', '["11, one per flag.", "9 of them.", "0, all rule artefacts.", "2, entries 70 and 170."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The Hampel module: entries 70 and 170 are the planted spikes, and the other 9 are not planted defects. Each of those 9 is a sample farther from its window median than three scaled MADs of a seven-sample window, which is what the rule flags.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-a-moving-window', 6, 'Which setting in the Hampel table flags the fewest samples on the gamma ray?', '["halfWindow 10 with nSigma 3: 4 flags.", "halfWindow 5 with nSigma 5: 3 flags.", "halfWindow 3, nSigma 5: 6 flags.", "halfWindow 5 with nSigma 3, whose 11-sample window returns the fewest flags of all."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Hampel module prints 11, 8, 6, 11, 3 and 4 flags down the table; halfWindow 5 at nSigma 5 gives the smallest count, 3. halfWindow 10 at nSigma 3 gives 4, halfWindow 3 at nSigma 5 gives 6, and halfWindow 5 at nSigma 3 gives 11.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-a-moving-window', 7, 'Does any setting in the Hampel table flag the two planted spikes and nothing else?', '["No. The smallest count is 3, and both spikes are flagged at every setting.", "Yes: halfWindow 10 with nSigma 3 flags exactly entries 70 and 170.", "Yes: halfWindow 5 with nSigma 5 flags only the two planted spikes.", "No, because at nSigma 5 the spikes themselves drop out of the flags."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The Hampel module''s settings table reads true in the both-spikes column on every row, and its smallest flag count is 3, at halfWindow 5 and nSigma 5. halfWindow 10 at nSigma 3 flags 4, so every setting flags at least one sample beyond the two spikes.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-a-moving-window', 8, 'What does the engine return for gamma ray entry 0 at halfWindow 3?', '["No result for an edge sample.", "A window padded to 7 samples with copies of entry 0, then judged in the usual way.", "A window of 4 samples, judged true, and no flag.", "A window of 4, judged false."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The Hampel module: at entry 0 the window is truncated to 4 samples, `windowCount` 4, which is at least three, so it is judged and not flagged. The engine truncates at the ends and pads nothing.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-a-moving-window', 9, 'On the EKENE-7 density, entry 80 is the first sample of the twelve-sample gap. What does `hampel` return for it?', '["A flag, since its window holds no present value and the MAD is zero.", "`judged` false: a missing sample is not judged.", "The window median of its neighbours, written in as a replacement.", "`judged` true against the 4 present samples beside it."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Hampel module: entry 80 is missing and is not judged (`judged` false). Missing values never enter a window, and the engine''s export list says the engine does not fill a value. Entry 79, beside the gap, is the one whose window holds 4 present samples.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-a-moving-window', 10, 'On 2.3, null, null, 2.9, null, null, 2.31 with halfWindow 1, what does the engine report for entry 3?', '["3 samples, judged true.", "A refusal naming `values`.", "A flag: 2.9 stands apart.", "1 present sample in its window, and `judged` false."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The Hampel module: entry 3 has 1 present sample in its window and `judged` false, because a window with fewer than three present samples is not judged. Missing values never enter a window, and the engine raises no refusal here.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-a-moving-window', 11, 'The golden case hampel-on-the-threshold runs -1, 0, 1.482600, 1, 0 at halfWindow 2 and nSigma 1. Which entry does it flag?', '["Entry 4", "Entry 2", "Entries 2 and 4", "None"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The Hampel module: entry 2 sits exactly on its threshold (window median 0, MAD 1, threshold 1.482600) and is not flagged, since the comparison is strict; the case flags entry 4.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-a-moving-window', 12, 'Hampel flags gamma ray entry 170 and returns a `cleaned` series. What happens to the original reading of 90.590000?', '["The engine overwrites it in the input with the window median, since a flagged value is a known error.", "It is deleted from the series, and the window medians around it are recomputed without it.", "It stays in the input; the cleaned series carries 38.410000, and using it is the caller''s decision.", "It is replaced by the mean of the whole channel, which the global z-score had already computed."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The Hampel module gives the replacement as the window median, 38.410000 for entry 170. The engine''s export list: the engine does not fill, repair or delete a value, and `hampel` returns a cleaned series beside its flags; choosing to use it is the caller''s decision.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-a-moving-window', 13, 'Asked for a Hampel screen with halfWindow set to 0, how does the engine respond?', '["A window of one sample, which flags nothing because its MAD is zero.", "A refusal naming `halfWindow`: it must be a whole number, 1 or more.", "The default halfWindow of 3, recorded in the basis block as a substitution.", "A result in which every sample is judged false and none is flagged."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The refusal table lists the refusal: "halfWindow must be a whole number, 1 or more (the window is 2 x halfWindow + 1 samples)". The engine refuses by name and does not fall back to a halfWindow of 3.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-a-moving-window', 14, 'The decision inside `hampel` is imported from the petrophysics engine''s `despikeHampel`, whose own entry point turns a null into 0. What does that do to this engine''s results?', '["Every missing sample enters its window as a zero and drags the window median down with it.", "Missing samples are flagged, since a zero sits far from the window median of a gamma ray log.", "The engine refuses any series with a missing value, so the conversion never happens at all.", "Nothing: `hampel` converts missing values to NaN before the call."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine notes: `hampel` converts missing values to NaN before the call, so this engine is unaffected, and the Hampel module confirms missing values never enter a window. The density gap is handled without a refusal, entry 80 simply not judged.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-a-moving-window', 15, 'At nSigma 3, the window is widened from halfWindow 3 to halfWindow 5. What happens to the flag count on the gamma ray?', '["It stays at 11.", "It falls to 3, since a wider window holds a steadier median.", "It rises, since more neighbours bring more chances to stand apart.", "It falls to 4, the count at the widest window in the table."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Widening the window alone leaves the count unchanged at 11 while nSigma stays at 3. The drop to 3 flags needs nSigma 5 as well, and the count of 4 comes from the twenty-one sample window.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-formal-tests-and-many-variables', 1, 'What is Grubbs'' G on the fourteen EKENE-7 core plugs with the one fractured plug?', '["2.507321", "3.611249", "2.985356", "5.890633"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The Grubbs lesson prints G 2.985356, which equals the largest |z| of the z-score module. 2.507321 is the two-sided critical value at alpha 0.05, 3.611249 is the t point used to build it, and 5.890633 is the plugs'' largest modified z-score.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-formal-tests-and-many-variables', 2, 'For the two-sided test on the fourteen plugs at alpha 0.05, which t point does the engine use to build the critical value?', '["The upper 0.001786 point of Student''s t on 12 degrees of freedom.", "The upper 0.05 point on 13 df.", "The upper 0.025 point on 14 df.", "The upper 0.001786 normal point."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The Grubbs lesson: t is the upper alpha / (2N) point of Student''s t on N - 2 degrees of freedom, and the table prints the tail probability 0.001786 and t 3.611249 for N = 14. Dividing alpha by 2N is the correction for testing the largest of N values.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-formal-tests-and-many-variables', 3, 'The two-plug copy of the core set faces the same critical value, 2.507321, as the one-plug set. Why?', '["The engine caches the first critical value it computes and reuses it for every later call on core data.", "The two sets have the same mean.", "The second high plug sits exactly on the critical value, so the engine holds the value fixed there.", "The critical value depends on n, alpha and the side only, and all three are unchanged."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The Grubbs lesson: the critical value depends on n, alpha and the side only; the values the rows were run on do not enter it. Both sets hold fourteen plugs, tested two-sided at 0.05.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-formal-tests-and-many-variables', 4, 'With a second high plug at entry 2, what does Grubbs'' test report on the fourteen plugs?', '["G 2.985356 above 2.507321, with entry 2 as the suspect.", "G 2.275359 below the critical 2.507321, so no rejection.", "G 2.275359 above a critical value that fell to 2.371654.", "A refusal, since the test is for one outlier and the set holds two."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Grubbs lesson''s masking row: sample SD 0.024800, G 2.275359, critical 2.507321, reject false, suspect entry 8. 2.371654 is the one-sided critical at n = 14, and the engine runs the test with no refusal.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-formal-tests-and-many-variables', 5, 'In the masking example, what moves G from 2.985356 down to 2.275359?', '["The critical value rises.", "The suspect switches to entry 2.", "The second plug raises the sample SD from 0.019859 to 0.024800.", "The engine drops the most extreme plug and tests the next one, as a generalised ESD test would."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The Grubbs lesson: with a second high plug the sample SD rises from 0.019859 to 0.024800, and G falls to 2.275359. The critical value stays 2.507321, the suspect stays entry 8, and the generalised ESD test is not built.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-formal-tests-and-many-variables', 6, 'What is the one-sided critical value for Grubbs'' test at n = 14 and alpha 0.05?', '["2.371654", "2.507321", "3.474396", "2.031652"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The Grubbs lesson''s table prints 2.371654 one-sided and 2.507321 two-sided at n = 14. 3.474396 is the largest G fourteen values can produce, and 2.031652 is the one-sided critical at n = 8.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-formal-tests-and-many-variables', 7, 'How does the two-sided Grubbs critical value at alpha 0.05 move across the table''s sample sizes, from 6 to 30?', '["It falls, from 2.908473 to 1.887145, since a larger sample makes the estimate steadier.", "It holds near 3 at every size, the same bar the z-score uses.", "It tracks the largest possible G row by row, since the critical value is that ceiling.", "It rises, from 1.887145 to 2.908473."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The Grubbs lesson prints 1.887145 at n = 6 rising to 2.908473 at n = 30. The largest possible G runs from 2.041241 to 5.294651 over the same rows, above the critical value at every n.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-formal-tests-and-many-variables', 8, 'At ten values the z-score cannot pass 3, since its ceiling is 2.846050. Can Grubbs'' test reject at ten values and alpha 0.05, two-sided?', '["No, since Grubbs'' G is the largest |z| and so shares the z-score''s ceiling of 3.", "No: the engine refuses Grubbs'' test below eleven values.", "Yes: its critical value is 2.289954, below the ceiling of 2.846050.", "Only one-sided, at 2.176068."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The Grubbs lesson prints the two-sided critical 2.289954 and the largest possible G 2.846050 at n = 10. G shares the ceiling, and the bar it must clear is 2.289954 in place of 3, so a rejection is reachable one-sided (2.176068) or two-sided.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-formal-tests-and-many-variables', 9, 'What does the engine offer for a sample that may hold several outliers?', '["The generalised ESD test, run automatically whenever Grubbs rejects its first suspect.", "Grubbs for one outlier only; the generalised ESD test is not built.", "Grubbs repeated, removing each suspect.", "A robust covariance, which the engine applies to any single-channel series."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Grubbs lesson: the test is for ONE outlier; NIST points to the generalised ESD test for several, which the engine does not build. The engine notes repeat it in the list of what is not built, beside the robust covariance.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-formal-tests-and-many-variables', 10, 'What cutoff does `mahalanobis` compare squared distances with on the EKENE-7 oil sand, density and neutron, at the default alpha?', '["7.377759", "5.991465", "9.210340", "22.397696"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The Mahalanobis lesson: the default alpha is 0.025 and the cutoff is the chi-square 0.975 quantile on 2 degrees of freedom, 7.377759. 5.991465 is the cutoff at alpha 0.05, 9.210340 at 0.010000, and 22.397696 is entry 60''s squared distance.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-formal-tests-and-many-variables', 11, 'Oil sand entry 60 reads z -1.074527 on density and -1.645167 on neutron, and neither channel''s z-score flags anything. Why does `mahalanobis` flag it at 22.397696?', '["The pair sits off the density-neutron trend, which only a distance that knows the correlation sees.", "Its density alone is beyond the sand''s range, and the distance simply adds the two z-scores together.", "The distance uses the population covariance, which inflates every row''s distance above the z-scores.", "Entry 60 sits beside the density gap, and rows next to a gap are weighted up in the covariance."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The Mahalanobis lesson: each value alone is ordinary, and the pair sits off the trend; the correlation, -0.836264, is what the distance knows. The covariance is the sample one (n - 1), and a density and a neutron inside their sand ranges are what the generator planted.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-formal-tests-and-many-variables', 12, 'Of the 60 oil sand rows, how many enter the Mahalanobis calculation, and why?', '["60: missing densities are filled with the column mean before the covariance is built.", "50: the gap''s two edge rows are kept, carrying a neutron value alone.", "48: rows with a missing value, EKENE-7 entries 80 to 91, are skipped and listed.", "12: only rows beside the density gap are used, so the covariance reflects the pad lift."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The Mahalanobis lesson: 60 rows, 48 complete rows used, and the skipped entries are 80 to 91, the twelve-sample density gap. The engine fills nothing, and rows with a missing value are skipped and listed.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-formal-tests-and-many-variables', 13, 'At alpha 0.05 the cutoff on 2 degrees of freedom is 5.991465. Which oil sand rows are flagged?', '["Entry 60 alone, since the flag count does not move with alpha.", "Entries 60 and 62.", "Entries 60, 62 and 56.", "Entry 62 alone, since 60 is masked."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Mahalanobis lesson: at 0.050000 two rows are flagged. Entry 62''s d^2 of 6.581737 is above 5.991465 and entry 60''s 22.397696 is too; entry 56, at 5.442663, comes in only at alpha 0.100000 with the cutoff 4.605170.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-formal-tests-and-many-variables', 14, 'What covariance does `mahalanobis` use, and what does its basis block call it?', '["A robust covariance that down-weights rows far from the centre, labelled robust.", "The population covariance (n), labelled classical, as the chi-square cutoff assumes.", "A covariance from the rows below the cutoff only, refitted until no row is flagged.", "The sample covariance (n - 1) with the classical mean, labelled classical and not robust."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The Mahalanobis lesson: d^2 uses the classical mean and the SAMPLE covariance S (n - 1), and a robust covariance is not built. The engine notes quote the label "sample covariance (n - 1), classical (not robust)".'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-formal-tests-and-many-variables', 15, 'A caller passes four rows in which the neutron is exactly twice the density. What does `mahalanobis` return?', '["A refusal naming `rows`: the covariance matrix is singular.", "A distance of zero for every row, since the rows lie on one line.", "A refusal naming `alpha`, since the chi-square cutoff needs more rows.", "Four flags, one per row, because the correlation is perfect."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The refusal table lists the refusal for one variable a multiple of the other, field `rows`: "rows have a singular covariance matrix: a variable is constant or one is a linear combination of others". Four rows meet the minimum of p + 2 = 4, so the singular matrix is what refuses.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-outliers-in-a-report', 1, 'On the EKENE-3 gauge, which method flags entry 1 as well as entry 7 at its defaults?', '["The modified z-score, which reads entry 1 against the median.", "The Hampel window, at halfWindow 3.", "Tukey''s fences on R7 quartiles at k 1.5.", "Grubbs'' test, two-sided at alpha 0.05."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The method comparison''s gauge row reads 7 for the modified z-score, 7 for the fences, 1 and 7 for Hampel and 7 for Grubbs. Only the moving window adds entry 1.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-outliers-in-a-report', 2, 'On the core plugs with two high plugs, which methods flag nothing at all?', '["The modified z-score and Tukey''s fences.", "Hampel and the z-score.", "All five, since the two plugs mask each other.", "The z-score and Grubbs'' test."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The method comparison: on the two-plug copy the z-score and Grubbs return none, while the modified z-score, the fences and Hampel each flag entries 2 and 8. The methods that measure from a mean and SD the plugs help set are the ones masked.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-outliers-in-a-report', 3, 'On the single fractured plug set, which of the five methods in the comparison table misses entry 8?', '["The z-score beyond 3.", "Grubbs'' test, since G stops short of 3 on fourteen plugs.", "The Hampel window, since plugs have no neighbours in depth.", "Tukey''s fences, since the IQR widens around a high plug."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The method comparison''s core row reads none for z beyond 3, and 8 for the modified z-score, the fences, Hampel and Grubbs. Grubbs compares G 2.985356 with 2.507321 and rejects.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-outliers-in-a-report', 4, 'According to the course''s comparison, what does the Hampel window measure a value against?', '["A mean and SD that the value helps set.", "A two-channel correlation.", "A local window of its neighbours.", "A median and quartiles over the whole series."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The method comparison: z and Grubbs measure against a mean and SD the outlier helps set, the modified z and the fences against a median and quartiles it barely moves, Hampel against a local window and Mahalanobis against a correlation.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-outliers-in-a-report', 5, 'NIST prints the uranium example''s Grubbs G as 2.4687; the engine returns 2.468765. What does the difference show?', '["The engine''s G is wrong in the fourth decimal, since the golden anchor is the NIST page itself.", "The printed figure was truncated rather than rounded: rounded to four decimals the engine reads 2.4688.", "NIST used the population SD, which moves G down in the fourth decimal on eight values.", "The engine rounds G down to protect the one-sided test from a false rejection at the edge."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine notes table the Grubbs G erratum: printed 2.4687, engine 2.468765, truncated rather than rounded, with 2.4688 as the four-decimal rounding. It is a note about a published page, and the engine reproduces the printed figure within the stated allowance.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-outliers-in-a-report', 6, 'The population case of nine zeros and a one at threshold 2.9 returns a flag whose `statistic` field reads 3.000000 at six decimals. What does this course quote in a report?', '["The reason string, because it carries every digit and is the more exact of the two figures.", "Whichever of the two is shorter, since both describe the same computed statistic.", "Neither: a flag from the population SD is never quoted in a report.", "The numeric field at six decimals, with the reason quoted only verbatim as the engine''s words."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine notes: a reason prints every figure as the shortest decimal that reads back to the field it quotes, so a computed statistic prints every digit. A lesson quotes the field at six decimals, 3.000000 here, and quotes a reason only as the engine''s own words.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-outliers-in-a-report', 7, 'What is the uranium example''s Grubbs result in the engine, one-sided max at alpha 0.05?', '["G 2.468765 against a critical 2.031652, so the test rejects.", "G 2.468765 against a critical 2.126645, so the test rejects.", "G 2.468765 against a critical 2.474874, so it does not reject.", "G 2.4687 against a critical 2.032, so it does not reject."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The Grubbs lesson prints G 2.468765 against the printed 2.4687 and the critical 2.031652 against the printed 2.032, reject true. 2.126645 is the two-sided critical at n = 8 and 2.474874 the largest possible G at eight values.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-outliers-in-a-report', 8, 'Which of these does the engine build?', '["A generalised ESD test for several outliers", "A robust covariance for the Mahalanobis distance", "Grubbs'' test for one outlier", "A fallback spread for when the MAD is zero"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine notes'' list of what is not built holds the generalised ESD test, a robust covariance and a fallback for a MAD of zero, the last answered with a refusal. Grubbs for one outlier is built, with the masking it suffers.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-outliers-in-a-report', 9, 'A flag from any of this tier''s rules comes back on an entry. Which statement about it matches the engine?', '["The value is wrong, since each rule is calibrated so that flags fall only on bad data.", "The rule fired, with its reason; the engine does not decide whether the value is wrong.", "The value has been removed from the series, and the result reports what is left.", "The value is a planted defect, since the Ekene series were built to carry them."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine''s export list: the engine does not decide whether a flagged value is wrong; a flag is a rule that fired, with its reason, and the engine does not fill, repair or delete a value. On the gamma ray, 9 of the 11 Hampel flags are not planted defects.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-outliers-in-a-report', 10, 'In the oil sand at alpha 0.05, entry 62 is flagged beside entry 60. What is entry 62?', '["A planted defect, set off the density-neutron trend as entry 60 was.", "A row skipped for a missing density, listed beside the flagged rows.", "A duplicate of entry 60, carried into the flags by the covariance.", "A sand row flagged by the stated cutoff, with no planted defect behind it."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The Ekene dataset table lists entry 60 as the Mahalanobis defect and no other oil sand row. Entry 62''s d^2 of 6.581737 is above the alpha 0.05 cutoff of 5.991465, so it is flagged by the rule at that setting; the skipped rows are entries 80 to 91.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-outliers-in-a-report', 11, 'Which of these is an examination that the rule actually made and passed?', '["A z-score run with `thresholdReachable` false and zero flags.", "A Hampel point with `judged` false and no flag.", "A point the Hampel rule judged, with no flag raised.", "A Mahalanobis row skipped for a missing value."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The Hampel module: `judged` is true when a window holds three or more present samples, so a judged, unflagged point was examined and passed. A z run with `thresholdReachable` false could not flag anything, an unjudged point was never examined, and a skipped row never entered the distance.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-outliers-in-a-report', 12, 'In this course, what does the word outlier have to carry?', '["The stated rule that flagged the value.", "A P label saying which tail the value sits in.", "Proof that the value is a bad measurement.", "The deletion that followed it in the dataset."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The course vocabulary: an outlier is a value a stated rule flags, and the rule is named; a flag is a question about the value. No P label is used anywhere in the course, and the engine deletes nothing.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-outliers-in-a-report', 13, 'A report quotes a spread built from a Hampel window. How does the course require it to be named?', '["As a bare sigma, since every window reads in SD units.", "As 1.4826 x MAD, with the window it came from.", "As the raw MAD, which Hampel uses unscaled.", "As the sample SD of the whole channel."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The course vocabulary lists the sources a sigma may carry: sample SD, MRbar / 1.128, 1.4826 x MAD, or historical in-control data. The Hampel module builds the Hampel threshold on nSigma x 1.4826 x MAD of the window, so the raw MAD is the modified z-score''s spread.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-outliers-in-a-report', 14, 'A caller''s two variables have very small variances in their own units, and `mahalanobis` refuses them as singular. What does the course say about it?', '["The variables must be linear combinations of each other, since the refusal is never raised otherwise.", "The engine has converted the units, so the caller should pass the variables in SI units.", "The singular check uses an absolute pivot test; rescaling the variables avoids the refusal.", "The refusal means there are fewer than p + 2 complete rows, and more rows will clear it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine notes: the refusal for a singular covariance uses an absolute pivot test inside lib/linalg, so variables with very small variance in the caller''s units can be refused as singular, and rescaling avoids it. The engine converts no units, and too few rows raise a different refusal.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-outliers-in-a-report', 15, 'NIST prints the silicon wafer 0.9 quantile as 95.1981 by R6, 95.1957 by R7 and 95.1972 by R8. How do the engine''s figures compare?', '["They agree with NIST only for R6, the rule NIST uses in its handbook.", "They differ in the third decimal, since NIST truncates each quantile.", "They match NIST only after the engine is switched to R6.", "Each equals its golden, and each rounds to the figure NIST printed."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The quantile rules lesson prints 95.198070, 95.195680 and 95.197243, each with a relative difference of 0 against its golden, and each rounds to 95.1981, 95.1957 and 95.1972. The truncation erratum belongs to the Grubbs G.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 1, 'Gauge entry 7 reads 2.845783 by the z-score and 186.162000 by the modified z-score. What accounts for the gap?', '["The modified z-score squares each deviation from the median before it divides by the MAD.", "The z-score defaults to the population SD, which damps the distance of every reading.", "The z divides by a sample SD the glitch inflated to 8.732220; the MAD of 0.100000 is untouched by it.", "The modified z-score counts the glitch twice, once in the median and once in the MAD."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The median and MAD module: the median is 212.500000 and the MAD 0.100000 with or without entry 7, while the sample SD is 8.732220 with it and 0.126930 without. The z default is the sample SD, and the modified z-score is linear in the deviation.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 2, 'On the fourteen core plugs the z-score flags nothing and Grubbs'' test rejects, yet both measure the same 2.985356. What differs?', '["The line it is compared with: a fixed 3 for the z-score, a critical 2.507321 for Grubbs.", "The spread: Grubbs recomputes the SD with the suspect plug left out before it divides.", "The centre: Grubbs measures from the median of the plugs, which the fractured plug cannot move.", "The sample: Grubbs tests the thirteen ordinary plugs and compares entry 8 with them."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The Grubbs lesson: G is the largest |Y - mean| / s with the sample SD, 2.985356 on these plugs, the same as the largest |z| of the z-score module. The z-score compares it with 3; Grubbs with 2.507321 for n = 14 at alpha 0.05, two-sided.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 3, 'On the two-plug copy, the modified z-score flags entries 2 and 8 while Grubbs'' test rejects nothing. Why do the two families part?', '["The modified z-score uses a lower threshold than Grubbs'' critical value of 2.507321.", "Grubbs'' test refuses a set with two outliers in it, so it returns a null result there instead.", "The modified z-score runs on the population SD, which the second plug cannot move.", "Two plugs barely move a median, while the second plug lifts the sample SD to 0.024800."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The method comparison: the modified z and the fences measure against a median and quartiles the outliers barely move, and z and Grubbs against a mean and SD they help set. The Grubbs lesson prints the sample SD rising from 0.019859 to 0.024800 with the second plug, and G falling below the critical value.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 4, 'At R7 and k 1.5, which gauge entries do Tukey''s fences flag?', '["Entries 1 and 7", "Entry 7 alone", "No entry", "Entry 1 alone"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The method comparison''s gauge row reads 7 under Tukey fences, R7 k 1.5. Entries 1 and 7 is the Hampel cell, and the z-score is the method that flags none.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 5, 'Which two runs catch the gamma ray spike at entry 170?', '["Tukey''s fences on the whole log, and the z-score on the whole channel.", "The z-score on the whole channel, and the Hampel window on the same channel.", "Tukey''s fences on the water sand alone, and the Hampel window on the whole channel.", "Only the Hampel window, since no global rule can see a spike inside a sand."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The fences lesson: on the water sand the default fences flag entry 170, and on the whole log they flag 0. The Hampel module: Hampel flags entry 170 at halfWindow 3 and nSigma 3, while the z-score on the whole channel reads 0.900269 and flags nothing.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 6, 'Entry 170 reads z 0.900269 on the whole gamma ray channel. Where does its reading of 90.590000 sit against the water sand''s default fences?', '["Inside the upper fence, since a z below 3 cannot cross a fence.", "Beyond the R6 upper fence of 49.117500 alone, and inside the R7 fence.", "Inside, because the fences are built on the whole log''s spread.", "Beyond the upper fence of 48.685000, so it is flagged."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The fences lesson prints the default R7, k 1.5 fences on the water sand as 21.905000 and 48.685000, flagging entry 170. 90.590000 lies beyond both the R7 and the R6 upper fence; the whole-log fences, -57.070000 and 186.810000, belong to a different run.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 7, 'Which statement about the constants 0.6745 and 1.4826 is correct?', '["The engine computes 0.6745 as 1 / 1.4826 at run time, so the two are exact reciprocals.", "The modified z uses 0.6745 as printed and Hampel uses 1.4826, so the two are close without being exact reciprocals.", "Both come from the petrophysics conditioning engine, which is why they pair so neatly.", "The modified z-score divides by 1.4826 x MAD, and Hampel multiplies the raw MAD by 0.6745."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The median and MAD module: 1 / 1.4826 is 0.674491, and the engine uses 0.6745 as printed, from Iglewicz and Hoaglin. The engine''s export list gives 1.4826 as the conditioning engine''s `HAMPEL_MAD_SCALE`, and the Hampel module multiplies the raw MAD by it.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 8, 'Two golden cases place a value exactly on its line: -4 and 12 on Tukey fences, and entry 2 at 1.482600 on its Hampel threshold. Which does the engine flag?', '["None of them.", "All three, since reaching a line counts as crossing it.", "The fence values -4 and 12, and not the Hampel entry.", "The Hampel entry, and neither fence value."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The fences lesson: iqr-exactly-on-both-fences returns 0 flags; the Hampel module: entry 2 sits on its threshold and is not flagged, the case flagging entry 4. A value strictly outside a fence, or strictly beyond a threshold, is what fires.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 9, 'Eight values cannot pass a z of 3 with the sample SD. What two-sided Grubbs critical value do they face at alpha 0.05?', '["2.031652", "2.126645", "2.474874", "2.468765"]'::jsonb, 1, 'For eight values the table''s two-tailed bar is 2.126645; the one-tailed bar, 2.031652, is the one NIST''s uranium example uses. The ceiling of 2.474874 is how far any G in eight values can reach, and 2.468765 is the uranium G itself.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 10, 'Which comparison decides the Hampel flag at gamma ray entry 70?', '["Its z of 1.058062 on the whole channel against the z threshold of 3.", "Its reading of 95.420000 against the water sand''s upper fence of 48.685000.", "Its deviation of 58.420000 from the window median against a threshold of 34.248060.", "Its deviation of 52.180000 against a threshold of 14.766696 in its window."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The Hampel module''s row for entry 70: window median 37.000000, MAD 7.700000, threshold 34.248060 and a derived deviation of 58.420000. 52.180000 and 14.766696 belong to entry 170, and entry 70 sits in the oil sand, outside the water sand run.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 11, 'Keeping the seven-sample window, a caller asks for a sample to sit four scaled MADs out before it is flagged. How many gamma ray flags remain?', '["11 samples", "6 samples", "3 samples", "8 samples"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Moving nSigma from 3 to 4 at halfWindow 3 takes the count from 11 down to 8. A count of 6 needs nSigma 5 at that window, and 3 needs nSigma 5 with an eleven-sample window.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 12, 'Gamma ray entries 49 and 50 both carry a Hampel threshold of 6.582744. What do the two rows share?', '["The same reading, which puts the two of them the same distance from any median.", "The same window median, 36.540000, and the same window MAD, 1.480000.", "A window truncated at the top of the log, which fixes the threshold.", "A MAD of zero, which the engine replaces with a default spread."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Hampel module prints both rows with window median 36.540000 and MAD 1.480000; the threshold is nSigma x 1.4826 x MAD, so it matches. The readings differ, 24.810000 and 24.830000, and entries 49 and 50 sit far from the top of the log.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 13, 'Entry 30 reads 90.540000 gAPI against a window median of 96.290000. How can the Hampel rule flag a reading below its neighbours?', '["The test is on |x - window median|, and 5.750000 clears the threshold of 4.848102.", "It cannot; entry 30 is flagged by the z-score and reported through the Hampel output.", "Low readings are compared with a halved threshold, which 5.750000 clears.", "The engine flags any reading below its window median in a shale."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The Hampel module: a sample is flagged when |x - window median| > nSigma x 1.4826 x MAD, strictly, in either direction. Entry 30''s derived deviation is 5.750000 and its threshold 4.848102; the z-score on the channel flags nothing.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 14, 'At halfWindow 3, which is the first gamma ray entry whose window holds all 7 samples?', '["Entry 0", "Entry 6", "Entry 3", "Entry 1"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The Hampel module''s edge table prints `windowCount` 4, 5, 6 and 7 for entries 0 to 3. Entry 3 is the first with three samples on each side.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 15, 'Tightening alpha to 0.001000 moves the density and neutron cutoff up to 13.815511. What happens to the planted row?', '["It drops out, since the cutoff now sits above every squared distance.", "It is joined by entries 62 and 56, since a stricter alpha lets more rows in.", "Still flagged: 22.397696 lies beyond 13.815511, and no other row is.", "It is joined by entry 62."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The alpha table reads 1 row flagged at 0.001000. Entry 60''s d^2 of 22.397696 clears 13.815511 with room to spare, while entry 62, at 6.581737, needs a cutoff as loose as the one at 0.05.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 16, 'What centre does `mahalanobis` use for the oil sand density?', '["The classical mean, 2.280625 g/cm3.", "The median density, so entry 60 cannot pull it.", "A robust centre reweighted away from entry 60.", "Entry 60''s own density."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The Mahalanobis lesson prints the centre RHOB as 2.280625 g/cm3 from the classical mean, and states the classical estimates are pulled by outliers; a robust covariance is not built.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 17, 'The oil sand''s density and neutron have a derived correlation of -0.836264. What does that say about entry 60?', '["Density and neutron rise together, and entry 60 follows that trend.", "Density falls as neutron rises, and entry 60 reads low on both.", "The two channels are unrelated, so entry 60 is judged one channel at a time.", "Entry 60 is the row that makes the correlation negative in the first place."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Ekene dataset table: inside a sand density and neutron move against each other. Entry 60 reads RHOB 2.221000 against a centre of 2.280625 and NPHI 0.188000 against 0.243313, low on both, with z -1.074527 and -1.645167.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 18, 'A caller passes three complete rows of density and neutron to `mahalanobis`. What comes back?', '["Three squared distances with a warning that the covariance is uncertain.", "A refusal naming `alpha`, since the cutoff needs more degrees of freedom.", "A refusal naming `rows`: at least p + 2 = 4 complete rows are needed.", "A refusal for a singular covariance, since three points always lie in a plane."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The refusal table lists the refusal for too few rows, field `rows`: "rows need at least p + 2 = 4 complete rows for a sample covariance of 2 variables". Too few rows is the first refusal the three rows meet, and no distances come back.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 19, 'Meaning five percent, an analyst types alpha 5 into `grubbsTest`. How does the engine respond?', '["It divides by 100 and runs the test at 0.05.", "It runs the test at alpha 5 and rejects every sample.", "It clamps alpha to 1 and reports that nothing is rejected.", "It refuses, naming `alpha`: a significance level must lie strictly between 0 and 1."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The refusal table lists the refusal for alpha as a percentage, field `alpha`: "alpha must be a significance level strictly between 0 and 1". The engine refuses by name and does not divide the 5 by 100.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 20, 'Twenty values are screened with Grubbs'' test at alpha 0.05, looking in both directions. Which bar must G clear?', '["2.556581", "4.248529", "2.708246", "2.908473"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The critical table''s n = 20 row reads 2.708246 for both tails and 2.556581 for one. G itself can reach 4.248529 on twenty values, and 2.908473 belongs to thirty.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 21, 'In the two-plug copy, which entry does Grubbs'' test name as its suspect?', '["Entry 2", "Entry 8", "Both 2 and 8", "No suspect"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Grubbs lesson''s masking row names suspect entry 8 with reject false. The test names one suspect, the value farthest from the mean, whether or not it rejects.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 22, 'On the gauge, Grubbs'' test rejects entry 7 while `zScores` flags nothing, and G is the largest |z|. How can both be right?', '["Grubbs uses the population SD on the gauge, which lifts entry 7 above the z ceiling of 2.846050.", "The z-score was run with entry 7 left out, so its largest z belongs to an ordinary reading.", "Grubbs sets 2.845783 against a critical 2.289954 for ten values; the z-score sets it against 3.", "Grubbs rejects on the median, so it sees the glitch in the way the modified z-score does."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The Grubbs lesson: G is the largest |z| with the sample SD, and the two-sided critical at n = 10 is 2.289954. The z-score module gives entry 7''s z as 2.845783, below 3; the method comparison has Grubbs rejecting entry 7 on the gauge.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 23, 'Hampel flags gauge entry 1 as well as the glitch. Which description of entry 1 does the course support?', '["A second planted glitch, placed to test whether a method can find two.", "A reading the engine has marked wrong and replaced in the input.", "A sentinel value that completeness counts as present.", "A reading the rule flags that is not the planted glitch, which is entry 7."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The Ekene dataset table plants one gauge defect, at entry 7, and the method comparison shows Hampel flagging 1 and 7. The engine''s export list: the engine does not decide a flagged value is wrong and does not repair it.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 24, 'At alpha 0.100000 three oil sand rows are flagged. Which row joins entries 60 and 62?', '["Entry 56, at 5.442663 against a cutoff of 4.605170.", "Entry 80, the first density gap row, now counted.", "Entry 70, the gamma ray spike in the oil sand.", "Entry 61, the row beside the planted row at entry 60 in depth."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The Mahalanobis lesson prints entry 56''s d^2 as 5.442663, the third largest, and the cutoff at 0.100000 as 4.605170. Entries 80 to 91 are skipped rows, and the gamma ray is not an input to this distance.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 25, 'Two analysts quote 48.685000 and 186.810000 as the R7, k 1.5 upper gamma ray fence, and both ran the engine correctly. What differs?', '["The quartile rule: one used R7 and the other R6 on the same series.", "The series: the water sand alone against the whole log, shale included.", "The multiplier: one used k 1.5 and the other the outer fence multiplier of 3.", "The spread: one used the population SD in place of the sample SD."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Same rule, same k: 48.685000 comes from the seventy water sand samples and 186.810000 from the whole log with the shale in it. A switch to R6 would give 49.117500 on the sand, k 3 gives 58.727500, and a Tukey fence uses no standard deviation.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 26, 'What is the water sand gamma ray''s third quartile by R6?', '["38.642500", "38.725833", "38.737500", "31.817500"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Reading the third quartile column of the water sand table: R6 gives 38.737500, while R7''s 38.642500 and R8''s 38.725833 sit below it. The figure 31.817500 comes from the first quartile column.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 27, 'Excel, R and numpy share a default quantile rule. Which one is it, and what position does it use?', '["R6, with h = p(N + 1).", "R8, with h = p(N + 1/3) + 1/3.", "R7, with h = p(N + 1).", "R7, with h = 1 + p(N - 1)."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The quantile rules lesson: R7 is the default of Excel, R and numpy, with h = 1 + p(N - 1). p(N + 1) is R6''s position, NIST''s rule, and p(N + 1/3) + 1/3 is R8''s.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 28, 'On the fourteen core plugs, which pair is the centre and spread of the modified z-score?', '["Median 0.215500 and raw MAD 0.007500.", "Mean 0.221714 and sample SD 0.019859.", "Median 0.215500 and sample SD 0.019859.", "Mean 0.217154 and raw MAD 0.007500."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The median and MAD module prints the core median 0.215500 and MAD 0.007500. 0.221714 and 0.019859 are the mean and sample SD of the z-score module, and 0.217154 the mean with entry 8 left out.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 29, 'NIST''s uranium example has eight values, and the engine''s largest |z| is 2.468765. How close is that to what eight values allow?', '["Well past the ceiling, which the population SD lifts to 3 at eight values.", "Just under the ceiling of 2.474874, so no z in the set could pass 3.", "Level with the Grubbs critical value of 2.031652, which is the ceiling.", "Far below any ceiling, since the uranium values are close together."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The z-score module prints the uranium largest |z| as 2.468765 and the sample SD ceiling at n = 8 as 2.474874. 2.031652 is the one-sided Grubbs critical, a separate quantity, and the test rejects because G is above it.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 30, 'The engine''s reason for the gauge flag prints the modified z with every digit. Which figure does a report quote?', '["The reason''s full string of digits, since it is the more exact figure.", "186.159450, the figure the exact reciprocal of 1.4826 would give.", "2.845783, the z of the same reading, which needs no rounding.", "186.162000, the statistic field at six decimals."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The median and MAD module quotes the reason verbatim as the engine''s words and gives the field as 186.162000. The engine notes: a lesson quotes the field at six decimals and a reason only as the engine''s own words. 186.159450 is derived with 0.674491.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 31, 'Density and neutron should move against each other in a sand. Which method measures a row against that relationship?', '["The Hampel window, run on each channel", "Tukey''s fences on each channel", "The Mahalanobis distance", "Grubbs'' test on each channel"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The method comparison: Mahalanobis measures against a correlation. The Mahalanobis lesson: entry 60 is ordinary on each channel alone, with 0 flags from each single-channel z-score, and only the distance sees the pair.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 32, 'How should a report treat the nine gamma ray Hampel flags that are not planted spikes?', '["As flags from the stated rule, each a question about its sample.", "As engine errors, to be discarded before the report is written.", "As defects the generator planted without recording them.", "As proof that halfWindow 3 is the wrong window."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The course counts 9 Hampel flags on the gamma ray that were never planted as defects. The course''s vocabulary treats every one of them as a question for someone who knows the log, and nothing about them says the engine erred or that the window is badly set.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 33, 'The gauge''s two standard deviations are 8.732220 and 8.284111. Which one divides by n?', '["8.732220, the larger of the two.", "8.284111, the population SD.", "Neither; both divide by n - 1.", "8.732220, the sample SD."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The z-score module: the sample SD, which divides by n - 1, is 8.732220, and the population SD, which divides by n, is 8.284111. Dividing by the larger number gives the smaller spread.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 34, 'At R7 and k 3, where is the lower fence on the water sand gamma ray?', '["21.905000", "11.862500", "21.437500", "-57.070000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The fences lesson prints the R7, k 3 lower fence as 11.862500. 21.905000 is the R7, k 1.5 fence, 21.437500 the R6, k 1.5 fence, and -57.070000 the whole-log fence at the defaults.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 35, 'At n = 6 the two-sided Grubbs critical is 1.887145 and the largest possible G is 2.041241. What follows for a set of six values?', '["No rejection is possible, since G can never exceed the critical value.", "The test is refused below ten values, whatever G reads.", "A rejection is reachable, since the bar sits below what six values allow.", "Only a one-sided test can reject, at 1.822120."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The Grubbs lesson prints 1.887145 two-sided, 1.822120 one-sided and a largest possible G of 2.041241 at n = 6. The critical value sits below the ceiling on every row of the table, so a large enough G rejects either way.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 36, 'On the EKENE-7 density, entry 79 sits beside the twelve-sample gap. How many present samples does its window hold at halfWindow 3?', '["7 present samples", "3 present samples", "4 present samples", "1 present sample"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The gap side of entry 79''s window falls on missing densities, which never enter a window, leaving 4 present samples. Since that is at least three, the point is judged.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 37, 'The z-score misses both the gamma ray spike at entry 70 and the fractured core plug. Are the reasons the same?', '["Yes: both series are too short for the z ceiling to pass 3 with the sample SD.", "Yes: both are measured with the population SD, which damps every distance.", "No: the spike is negative, and the z-score looks only above the mean.", "No: the log''s spread is wide because shale and sand mix; the plug''s because the plug is in it."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The Hampel module: a sand spike looks like shale to a global mean and SD. The z-score module: the plug is measured against a spread that includes itself, 0.019859 against 0.010574 without it. Fourteen plugs could reach 3.474396, and the gamma ray log runs to 240 entries.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 38, 'Count the methods in the comparison table that find the gauge glitch at entry 7.', '["All five, since the glitch is the one planted defect in ten readings.", "Four of the five: all but the z-score.", "Two: the modified z-score and Tukey''s fences.", "Three: all but the z-score and Grubbs'' test."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Across the gauge row of the comparison only the z-score column is empty: the modified z-score, Tukey''s fences, the Hampel window and Grubbs'' test all list entry 7. Ten readings cap the sample SD z at 2.846050, under 3.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 39, 'On how many degrees of freedom is the chi-square cutoff taken for the density and neutron pair?', '["48, the complete rows used.", "60, the rows in the oil sand.", "2, the number of variables.", "1, one distance per row."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The chi-square quantile is taken on p degrees of freedom, p being the number of variables in each row. Density and neutron make p = 2; the count of rows, 48 used out of 60, sets the covariance estimate and leaves the degrees of freedom alone.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 40, 'NIST prints the uranium critical value as 2.032. How does it relate to the engine''s 2.031652?', '["It is the engine''s value rounded to three decimals.", "It is a different formula, one-sided where the engine is two-sided.", "It is truncated, the same way NIST prints the uranium G.", "It comes from a larger sample than the eight values the engine used."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The Grubbs lesson prints the engine''s critical 2.031652 against the printed 2.032 for the one-sided test, and the test rejects either way. The truncation erratum in the engine notes concerns the printed G, 2.4687.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 41, 'In the golden on-the-fence case, -4, 2, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 6, 12, what quartiles does R7 return?', '["Q1 -4 and Q3 12, the extreme values.", "Q1 3 and Q3 5, the inner values.", "Q1 2 and Q3 12, an IQR of 10.", "Q1 2 and Q3 6, an IQR of 4."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The fences lesson: the engine returns Q1 2, Q3 6 and IQR 4, which with k 1.5 put the fences at -4 and 12. -4 and 12 are the fences and the extremes of the data.'),
  ('dataqc', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 42, 'Hampel flags gamma ray entry 145. What does the `cleaned` series carry at that entry?', '["29.570000, the reading itself.", "36.810000, its window median.", "3.691674, the threshold.", "Nothing, since it is deleted."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Hampel module prints entry 145 with reading 29.570000, window median 36.810000 and replacement 36.810000. The cleaned series carries the replacement, and the engine deletes nothing.');
